# The Judge Became a Lawyer!
Tamil title: நீதிபதி வக்கீலானார்!

"You heard everything the witnesses said, did you not? Well, what do you say? Do you admit the theft or deny it?" asked the presiding judge.

The woman listened with delight. Her gaze even seemed filled with affection.

Her happiness resembled that of a daughter lovingly asked by her father, "Child, do you like this sari? Tell me."

Yet she stood in the prisoners' dock, and he was asking whether she had stolen. She was the thief; he the man who would punish her. Still she looked at him with a daughter's love.

He felt slightly embarrassed. Everyone else in court was bewildered. She stood entranced as though hearing music sweeter than any she had ever known: tenderness in her eyes, radiance upon her face, a smile upon her lips - all in a woman placed in the dock for theft.

The judge had never seen such a sight. He had seen people shed tears, fold their hands, and fall at his feet. Some had glared as though they would shoot him. Others begged: "Lord of justice, I am innocent. Fate dragged me here. I have children; do not punish me." Hardened fellows had said, "All right, sir. Would you believe me if I spoke? You keep asking when all these people have poisoned your mind. Would you believe I did no wrong?"

This was new and strange. Knowing he would punish her, she looked upon him with affection.

"Answer the question," he said more sternly.

Startled, she replied, "What must I say? I shall say it."

"The truth. The whole truth."

"You ask whether I stole. I did."

Something shifted in his mind. She did not deny it. She showed no anger, sorrow, or even shame. She announced "I stole" like someone proudly describing before an assembly an achievement worthy of praise. What kind of woman was this foolish girl?

A judge need not and should not consider matters unrelated to the case. Yet he could no longer follow that method, for pity had arisen in him. Fear, kindness, and partiality must not rule a court; the law must stand. He had followed that principle even when imposing death.

He had seen wives beat their breasts and cry, "Three years! May you perish! Sinner, is your heart stone?" He merely thought a woman naturally suffered when her husband received a severe sentence and therefore babbled.

This young woman showed neither anxiety nor confusion. Without hesitation she admitted stealing, displayed no sign of anger, and looked at him with affection. Was she mad? No; her answers were clear. Why then did she behave so?

For twenty years he had practised finding subtleties in law, not discerning states of mind or the nature of human thought. He had neither time nor inclination for that. Once spotless evidence established an offence, punishment was his duty. Yet that day he wanted somehow to save her.

Could a judge become a defence lawyer? He need not literally do so. He could question the witnesses again, probe whether falsehood had been made to look true, and conclude guilt was not beyond doubt. Because she was a young woman and a first offender, he might warn and release her without punishment.

But she was a young and attractive woman. What would the world say? Gossip was cultivated like an art. Lawyers would gather and laugh: "We thought him strict as fire, but one needs a fortunate face in every matter. Saint or renunciant, men become generous where women are concerned." Some already looked mischievously at him.

Though there was no reason, he asked again, "You are telling the truth - the whole truth?"

"I am telling the truth, but not the whole truth. It becomes whole only if I explain every reason this theft occurred. The case does not require that; indeed it should not. The law must do its work," she replied.

The judge's head seemed to spin. Police had reported that the accused spoke insolently in custody, which she did not deny. Now she spoke with perfect restraint - or rather, lectured like a schoolteacher.

"Three months' rigorous imprisonment," the judge ordered.

Everyone watched her. Neither anger nor sorrow crossed her face, and she seemed unashamed of imprisonment as a thief. Even after sentence she remained in the dock. The judge did not glare, and police did not drag her away.

"Sir," she called.

He had been turning the pages of a book merely to escape her gaze. Forgetting himself, he responded tenderly, "What is it, child?"

"The property I stole will be returned to its owner, will it not?"

With an indulgent, almost foolish smile, he said, "Mad girl, that is the law."

"If it reaches its owner, my mind will be at peace. Even prison will not seem cruel."

"You display such goodness now."

"I have always been good, sir."

"Do not speak so. Steal and then talk this way, and people will call you an accomplished criminal. Is theft the mark of goodness?"

"I do not argue that every theft arises from goodness. This theft alone is different. I did steal, but I am good. I stole because I am good."

"Is this a place for riddles?"

"I stayed silent because I knew you would say so. I speak now because my case is over, the law has been upheld, and you have completed your duty. Give the property to its real owner."

Until then the judge had been confused by her manner; now pity overwhelmed him. Surely some disorder afflicted her mind. Her formerly calm voice trembled, and tears gathered.

"We shall return it to Kathamuthu. You stole from his shop."

"Yes, I stole it there. But it is not Kathamuthu's."

"The theft occurred in his shop; the property is his."

"It happened to be there, but it did not belong to him. You asked for the whole truth. To assume everything in Kathamuthu's shop is his would not be the whole truth."

"You argue cleverly, but needlessly. You were seen taking it from his shop. Witnesses testified, and you do not deny it."

"You speak as though reopening the trial. The case and the judge's work have ended successfully and honourably. I have some education. My essay 'Learn Even Theft, Then Forget It' won a gold medal. Taking the object from Kathamuthu's shop remains theft even if it belonged to someone else. Yet return it to whoever truly owns it. That is not a judge's duty, perhaps, but..."

"Someone else may once have owned it, but Kathamuthu may have paid its proper price. Then it became his and must return to him."

"I have not studied law, so I do not know whether my words are legally correct. I speak because they are true, and law cannot reject the discovery of truth. Kathamuthu made the object legally his by paying money. Give him whatever price he asks and return it to the rightful owner."

"Who will pay?"

"I ask you to arrange it. Do you hear them laughing? Ask me no more here. They will clap and laugh."

The judge looked at the police, and they took her away.

The next case concerned a milk seller who mixed water into milk. Advocate Sadagopalachari began arguing that the law itself was meaningless because milk was a kind of water. But the judge's mind remained elsewhere.

He questioned Kathamuthu privately. The shopkeeper now pitied the girl and said he would not have handed her to police had he known she had some mental trouble.

"You heard her say the stolen article was not yours," said the judge.

"Does everything in a shop originally belong to its keeper? Various people sell things to me. I pay, polish them, and sell for a small profit. Every shop does that."

"From whom did you buy this object?"

"A broker named Nammalvar. I often buy different articles from him; he is no wrongdoer."

Pressed further, Kathamuthu asked protection and confessed that the medallion had been hidden inside another object. He paid Nammalvar only twenty rupees; had the broker known of the medallion, he would never have sold it so cheaply.

"The medallion is worth at least a thousand rupees."

"Yes, but Nammalvar sold me a theatrical mace without knowing the medallion was inside."

It was Bhima's silver-covered mace from the Pillaiyarappan Drama Company. Nathamuni, a tea-shop owner owed money by the troupe, had angrily taken and sold it to Nammalvar, who passed it to Kathamuthu.

The girl somehow knew the medallion was hidden there and offered up to a hundred rupees for the mace, though Kathamuthu had paid twenty. He refused even after she stood stubbornly before him and sent everyone away. That night he examined the mace, found nothing, and fell asleep. A crash woke him: she had split it into two and exposed the medallion. That was the theft witnesses saw.

The judge summoned the drama-company owner, who denied ever possessing such a medallion. Darkness closed around the investigation again.

He repeatedly recalled Muthurathinam's plea to return it to its owner. If it were hers, why not openly say so? She lacked money to buy such jewellery. Had she stolen it earlier and now suffered remorse? No explanation satisfied him.

At this time Dr Moses Thambi, superintendent of the mental hospital, came to see Judge Pathiriyappan. The telegram announcing his visit had already frightened the judge, who feared danger to his sister Muthazhagu.

Pathiriyappan had wealth and influence but no peace. His wife died of tuberculosis. Even earlier his sister's insanity had dealt him a great shock, striking just when her marriage was fixed. Their father Nallappar could not endure the blow, fell ill, and died. As Muthazhagu's illness worsened, confinement under medical supervision became unavoidable. Her care in the hospital gave Pathiriyappan his only consolation.

"There is no immediate danger," said the doctor. "But the condition is worrying. Still, I think there may be a cure."

"Tell me what must be done and where we must go."

The doctor handed him a letter:

"Doctor,

"I write from prison because I possess a heart.

"Ask the justice-knowing Pathiriyappan whether the medallion has been returned to its owner, as the foolish woman he sentenced requested.

"A man immersed in law may say: why inquire further? The thief is in prison, the property recovered. If so, take the medallion and show it to his sister Muthazhagu in your hospital.

"What your treatments could not achieve, this medallion will.

"The green and blue stones in it possess rare power.

"Show it to her. Do not tell him that tears have wet my letter; they may disturb his duty.

"Muthurathinam,

"living in prison."

The two hurried to the hospital.

Muthazhagu took the medallion, turned it repeatedly, and threw it upon the table. The doctor shook his head sadly. Pathiriyappan wept that he had come with such hope. The doctor called it a mad prisoner's letter; the judge said the medallion's only power was to send its thief to jail.

Muthazhagu's eyes returned to it. She examined the back, threw it down, then snatched it as her brother tried to pocket it. She rubbed the reverse against the floor, biting the doctor's finger when he intervened, and alternately rubbed and stared. The men thought it another mad act.

Suddenly she gave a joyful cry and danced with the medallion. With difficulty Pathiriyappan took it and saw the emblem of the Vennur estate on the reverse.

"Doctor!" he cried so wildly the physician feared the brother too had lost his mind. "The medallion is ours. I never imagined it. Here is the estate emblem. Father bought this jewel from the estate for Muthazhagu."

"Give it to me, brother," Muthazhagu clearly asked.

Both marvelled. She pressed it to her eyes and demanded to meet the person who had brought it.

"Soon, child," Pathiriyappan promised. "This is ours, is it not? Father bought it for you. It was with you. Did you give it to someone? Was it lost? Think carefully."

Her confusion had not wholly departed; she could not yet connect the returning memories. The doctor stopped further questions. Holding the medallion, she fainted, and he arranged her care while sending the judge home.

At his doorway Kathamuthu stopped him, cheeks red and finger marks visible.

"Do not enter. Send for the police. A murderer is inside, roaring like thunder. He struck me," he warned.

Pathiriyappan entered. A rough man had placed a chair upon a table and climbed it to remove Nallappar's portrait.

"Who are you, rogue? I shall summon police. Get down and give me the picture."

The man threw it down and shattered it.

"Sinner!" Pathiriyappan cried.

"Where is Muthazhagu?"

"What arrogance!"

"You are a gentleman and I a wanderer. You are educated and I a village brute. You are a judge and I a murderer. Do you understand? You sent Muthurathinam to prison, did you not? Only a human beast like you could see her guileless face and decide she was a thief. You are his son; how could your nature differ? Muthazhagu alone was born unlike you, and therefore suffered in your hands."

Shock followed shock. Pathiriyappan asked him to speak slowly and explain.

"That is the manner of your mansion. I do not need it. The law you studied gave you measured speech. The cruelty I saw and suffered made me a killer.

"I am Veeraiyan, who repaired your family's motorcars. I was imprisoned for a murder I did not commit because your law did not speak for me. Pandari took ten or twenty rupees and falsely testified that he saw me at the place. With vast law books before him, the lofty judge asked, 'What do you say?' just as you asked my daughter.

"'He lies, sir,' I said.

"The lawyer mocked me as a great truth-teller. I cried that I had not killed. 'Did the old woman kill herself?' the lawyer joked while the noose stood before my eyes.

"In court they asked whether I had evidence that I was elsewhere. Why could I not provide it? Because my tongue was ashamed. Evidence existed, but I feared exposing the scandal of a great household for the town to laugh at.

"Law may not distinguish great and small, but society does. Your law cannot change society's mind; it surrenders to it. They suppressed the peerless witness who could prove my innocence. I found only the portrait and shattered it. Had I found your father, I would have torn him apart. To preserve family honour, I was expected to become a murderer!"

The man described how conspiracy and cruelty had transformed him. Once he too had been human, refined, and loved by women - a young man worthy of Pathiriyappan's sister's heart.

"Yes, I am Veeraiyan. Are shame, grief, shock, and fear striking you together? Judge my case yourself and deliver a good verdict. They say you have a fine name."

Pathiriyappan said hidden truths had begun appearing like small sparks and begged for the full account.

"You wish me to tell and you to listen? You may not bear how many forms deceit takes. As a judge you will demand witnesses and evidence. What witnesses can I show?

"When Muthazhagu and I spoke secretly, a cow broke loose and made us run. It now nurses its calf. When she escaped my embrace complaining of my roughness, she threw broken bangles; how can I show them now? Once she held my shirt as I tried to leave; it tore, leaving a piece in her hand. Once I plucked her favourite fruit, and she threw a vine crying 'snake.' I stamped upon the actual snake and held it by the neck before her. There were a thousand witnesses, all vanished.

"But you saw the unfading witness and law blinded you. The maiden you sentenced for theft is the pure child born of Muthazhagu, who once rested upon my lap and said a small hut was enough for our life. What more evidence is needed?

"My life has two parts: honey shown to no one, and cruelty no one could endure. Your books describe offences, punishment, and trial. Do they explain how an offence is born? Do they show how a man renowned for goodness dares monstrous cruelty to protect false honour? Many events are buried, especially inside mansions. However wicked the acts, will they escape a fortress not of stone, but money?"

"Enough, Veeraiyan," Pathiriyappan said. "Your outline is enough. I understand that you suffered cruelty, that my father caused it, and that false honour and illness changed him too."

Veeraiyan had once tried to forget, but the same mansion's cruelty struck first him and then his daughter. His life had burned; Muthazhagu was in an asylum, and Muthurathinam, who should have graced the mansion, was in prison. Nallappar had sacrificed them all to family honour.

Veeraiyan explained that Muthazhagu had loved him despite every stratagem her father employed. Love was the joining of hearts, not lakhs of rupees or movement from one mansion to another.

Nallappar gave the wealth Veeraiyan had earned to an old woman in the settlement who helped hide the child. He trapped Veeraiyan in a false gambling case. Expecting a fine, Muthazhagu gave him the wedding medallion to sell. He instead kept the token of love close to his heart, intending it for their child.

After prison, Veeraiyan found that Nallappar and the old woman had conspired against the child. At eight years old, Muthurathinam fled like a bird. A drama troupe accepted her; there she sang, danced, and protected herself from danger.

Veeraiyan later found her and met Muthazhagu secretly in the mansion's cattle shed. Muthazhagu asked about prison. He hid its suffering, saying there was regular food and work. She accused him of telling neatly arranged lies. Playfully he pulled at the end of her sari, patterned with cashew fruit. She said it cost over sixty rupees and her father bought it. When she rose, he held on; the end tore into his hand. They laughed, and she tied the piece around his head like a turban.

Leaving that happiness, he entered an uproar in the settlement. An old woman had been killed. Men who heard him earlier threaten her falsely identified him. He told a departing prisoner named Velappan that Muthazhagu could prove his alibi: she could show the sari with its missing end, while he held the matching piece.

Velappan was a fearless highway robber and repeated prisoner, yet possessed a heart. He tried to help. Nallappar drove him away: would a gentleman announce his daughter loved such a brute? Muthazhagu fell at her father's feet and fought to tell the truth, but he dragged her into a room and locked her in.

The court demanded a witness. Veeraiyan possessed truth and a heart, but the town required acceptable proof. He hoped until the final moment she would break confinement and save him. The law did not slow for him. He received life imprisonment. That day Muthazhagu's mind broke.

Veeraiyan laboured in prison until even tears failed. On release he came to see his daughter and learnt that Pathiriyappan had sent her to prison. Deciding to commit a real murder and make prison his home, he demanded the medallion from Kathamuthu, beat him, and came to obtain it from the judge.

But Pathiriyappan's changed words opened another path.

"In two days our bright bird will leave prison," the judge said.

"When she comes, I shall take her to a distant place. I want no stain upon your name. Give me only the medallion, so somewhere I may remember this sinner once knew golden days."

"Those who commit cruelty are cruel, whoever they are," Pathiriyappan replied. "You have told me what my father did. Yet forgetting my sister, who lived carrying life for your sake, and proposing to leave with your daughter is itself cruel."

"She lives in my heart and will come with me. Her madness is a blessing; her confused mind will not weep for me."

"Her mind broke because she lost you."

"Because they separated us."

"Those are the same. Have you forgotten that you are the great medicine by which she may live?"

"I am her medicine and she my sweetest feast. But to the conventions guarded by the world, we are a great fire."

"Let them say so. Let narrow minds climb hills and shout. We shall declare that love accepts no divisions. One world binds itself and cries from pain; another, with new clarity and courage, makes time better. Is it not cowardice to flee when your world calls you?"

As they spoke, their hearts drew closer.

People were shocked when former Judge Pathiriyappan allowed life-sentenced Veeraiyan to stay in his home. The two received Muthurathinam at the prison gate and brought her home; the town hissed. When Muthazhagu returned fully restored from the hospital, it was a day of celebration.

Idlers boasted, hooligans threw stones, and legal experts wrote to higher authorities. Pathiriyappan answered them.

Soon the town said that anyone oppressed by caste difference or tormented by the powerful could find Advocate Pathiriyappan to argue for them. In a town where those above trampled and devoured the people below, he became their protector.

Some diamond-earring lawyers mocked that a man who should have risen far now lacked even money to hire assistants. But he needed none. Veeraiyan gathered hidden facts about cases; Muthurathinam prepared notes. Pathiriyappan argued not only what the law contained, but what it ought to contain. His speech came from the heart.

Lawyers who quarried books for strings of words lost cases. Pathiriyappan placed before the court different evidence: the poor man's tears, the labourer's sweat, the innocent person's sigh, the helpless woman's suffering.

The judge became a lawyer. Dry minds celebrated sarcastically.

Pathiriyappan did more than practise law. He undertook the work of removing the circumstances, anger, rivalry, and jealousy that gave birth to crime. Dominant men grew furious. Whenever fear touched him, he looked at Veeraiyan; courage surged, and his arguments became arrows that felled opponents.

"The judge became a lawyer for us," the settlement said.

"For you, yes," Pathiriyappan answered. "But not so you may continue committing offences while I defend you. I became a lawyer for justice: to create in you, and in the powers that seek to crush you, a mind and practice that respect justice."

When the powerful bent even law to their advantage and tried to obstruct and conceal justice, the judge became a lawyer to defeat them and establish it.

The legal circle continued crowing: "A life convict is his brother-in-law. The thief sent to prison is his sister's daughter. And the law permits such a man to speak in court!" But will a songbird cease its sweet speech because a few owls cry?

The former judge no longer had a motorcar. The mansion itself went to auction. Yet Pathiriyappan filled the hearts of the poor and stood guarding them.

"One Pathiriyappan is not enough," said the judge who had become a lawyer.

"Two years more, and I too shall join your side," said Muthurathinam.

"How can I trust you? Who knows whom you might rob?" Pathiriyappan teased.

"Mother, listen to the things he says!" Muthurathinam appealed to Muthazhagu for support.

"You ask that after choosing the right place," Muthazhagu laughed, and Pathiriyappan too joined the laughter.

*14 January 1961*

---NOTES---
The three numbered OCR sets form one continuous story. No page remained marked as missing, but the first title line, several transitional passages, and much of the final page contain damaged OCR; uncertain readings are recorded separately while the retained Tamil source remains unchanged.

The title is both event and argument: Judge Pathiriyappan literally leaves the bench to practise as an advocate, and his experience changes him from an official applying established law into a defender who asks what law and justice ought to become.

The medallion passes through Muthazhagu, Veeraiyan, the drama company's mace, broker Nammalvar, shopkeeper Kathamuthu, and Muthurathinam. This chain is retained because it connects the theft case to the older false-murder conviction and restores the hidden family history.

The source contrasts சட்டம் (law) with நீதி (justice), and ஆதாரம்/சாட்சி (evidence/witness) with lived signs such as tears, sweat, memory, and the torn sari. These distinctions are preserved consistently in the translation.
